.\" Man page for Vuurmuur 0.8.2. Kept in sync with the option handling in
.\" vuurmuur_log/vuurmuur_log.c (optstring, prog_opts and print_help()).
.TH "VUURMUUR_LOG" "8" "February 2025" "Vuurmuur 0.8.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vuurmuur_log \- log parsing daemon of the Vuurmuur IPTables frontend.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vuurmuur_log \fB[\-chnvVK]\fR \fB[\-c\fR \fIconfig\-file\fR \fB][\-d\fR \fIdebug\-level\fR \fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBvuurmuur_log\fR
turns what the kernel logged about matching packets into the records the
logviewer of
.BR vuurmuur_conf (8)
shows. It receives the packets from the netfilter log target, on the multicast
group named by \fBNFGRP\fR in the configuration file, and the connection
states from the netfilter connection tracking subsystem over netlink. It
resolves addresses and ports against the same objects
.BR vuurmuur (8)
installed, so a record names a host, a network and a service instead of bare
numbers.
.PP
It writes the traffic and connection logs itself; it reads no syslog file and
installs no syslog sink.
.PP
Unless \fB\-n\fR is given the process detaches with \fBdaemon\fR(3), called
with \fInochdir\fR and \fInoclose\fR set, so its standard streams stay
attached to the terminal it was started from.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the possible command line options and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose mode.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-nodaemon\fR
Do not detach: stay in the foreground.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-configfile\fR \fIfile\fR
Use \fIfile\fR instead of the default configuration file.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fIlevel\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR \fIlevel\fR
Enable debugging, 1 low, 3 high. Output goes to the debug log file, see
.B FILES
below. A level above the compiled in maximum aborts with an error.
.TP
\fB\-K\fR, \fB\-\-killme\fR
Ask the running daemon to stop by sending \fBSIGTERM\fR to the pid read from
the pid file, then exit. The short form takes no argument; the long form is
declared as needing one and rejects an argument-less use, so use \fB\-K\fR.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success, non zero on failure.
.SH "SIGNALS"
.TP
\fBSIGHUP\fR
Reload the configuration and the objects from the backend, and close and
reopen the log files. This is what the log rotation script relies on to hand
the log files over to their rotated names.
.TP
\fBSIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM\fR
Shut down cleanly, removing the shared memory segment and the pid file.
.PP
Note that \fBSIGHUP\fR sent to
.BR vuurmuur (8)
is not forwarded here, except when vuurmuur notices that a dynamic interface
changed address. Reload with
.BR vuurmuur_script (8)
\fB\-\-reload\fR, or signal both daemons.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vuurmuur/config.conf
Configuration file, unless another one was given with \fB\-c\fR. The settings
this daemon reads include \fBNFGRP\fR, \fBLOGDIR\fR and the connection
tracking options. The directory is fixed at build time unless
\fBVUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR\fR is set; see ENVIRONMENT.
.TP
.I /var/run/vuurmuur_log.pid
Pid file. It holds the process id and the id of the shared memory segment.
.TP
.I <logdir>/traffic.log
One line per logged packet. This is what the logviewer reads.
.TP
.I <logdir>/connections.log\fR, \fI<logdir>/connnew.log
Connection state records, from the connection tracking subsystem.
.TP
.I <logdir>/vuurmuur.log\fR, \fI<logdir>/error.log\fR, \fI<logdir>/debug.log
General, error and \fB\-d\fR output. Throughout, \fI<logdir>\fR is the
.B LOGDIR
setting of the configuration file, \fI/var/log/vuurmuur\fR unless that setting
says otherwise.
.PP
The log line \fBtraffic.log\fR carries is not the line the logviewer renders:
in the file an unresolved service reads \fI<srcport>*<dstport>(<proto>)\fR,
with the protocol as a number, and the interfaces are recorded as
\fIin:\ <dev>\fR and \fIout:\ <dev>\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR
Overrides the compiled-in configuration directory: the config file is then
.IR $VUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR /vuurmuur/config.conf
and the textdir backend's object tree moves with it, because the backend finds
it through the configuration. A warning is logged whenever the variable is in
use. Note that \fBLOGDIR\fR is read from that file, so the daemon starts
writing where the relocated configuration tells it to.
.SH HISTORY
The \fB\-s\fR and \fB\-N\fR letters are accepted and do nothing. This program's
option switch has no \fBdefault:\fR arm, so a misspelled flag leaves the logger
running on defaults rather than failing. The two shapes differ: a letter that is
not in the option string at all gets one line from getopt on stderr
(\fIinvalid option \-\- \'Z\'\fR) and is then skipped, while a letter that is in
the option string with no handler \- \fBs\fR and \fBN\fR \- leaves no trace
anywhere, not on stderr and not in the log files.
